Claims:
[
    "Drift refers to the changes that can occur in a machine learning model's performance or the data it processes over time, particularly after the model has been deployed.",
    "There are several types of drift, including data drift, model drift, and concept drift.",
    "Data drift happens when the statistical properties of the input data change.",
    "An example of data drift is when a model trained on data from a specific demographic may not perform well if the user base shifts to a different demographic.",
    "If a model was optimized for millennials, it might not meet the expectations of Gen Z users, leading to dissatisfaction and negative feedback.",
    "Model drift occurs when the model itself becomes less effective over time due to changes in the underlying data patterns or user behavior.",
    "Model drift can happen even if the data being fed into the model remains the same.",
    "Concept drift is a specific type of drift where the underlying relationships in the data change.",
    "If a model was originally designed to predict outcomes based on certain features, but those features become less relevant or are interpreted differently over time, the model's predictions may no longer align with user expectations or real-world outcomes.",
    "Understanding drift is crucial because it can lead to a decline in model performance, which ultimately affects user satisfaction and trust in the system.",
    "If a model does not adapt to changes, it may provide inaccurate or irrelevant results, prompting users to disengage or provide negative feedback.",
    "Monitoring for drift and implementing strategies to address it is essential for maintaining the effectiveness of AI systems after deployment."
] 

